===Etymology===
From {{etyl|bat-pro|lv}} {{m|bat-pro|*šuo}}, {{m|bat-pro|*šunas}} (parallel form {{m|bat-pro|*šuns}}), from {{etyl|ine-pro|lv}} {{m|ine-pro|*ḱwṓ}}, genitive {{m|ine-pro|*ḱunós}}, {{m|ine-pro|*ḱunés}}. An older {{etyl|lv|-}} form {{m|lv|*so}} is now found only in one dialect (Nīgrande) as a word to call dogs. Cognates include {{etyl|lt|-}} {{m|lt|šuõ}} (genitive {{m|lt|šuñs}}, dialectal {{m|lt|šunis}}), {{etyl|prg|-}} {{m|prg|sunnis|sunis}}, {{etyl|gem-pro|-}} {{m|gem-pro|*hundaz}} ({{etyl|got|-}} {{m|got|𐌷𐌿𐌽𐌳𐍃}}, {{etyl|non|-}} {{m|non|hundr}}, {{etyl|is|-}} {{m|is|hundur}}, {{etyl|sv|-}} {{m|sv|hund}}, {{etyl|goh|-}} {{m|goh|hunt}}, {{etyl|ang|-}} {{m|ang|hund}}, {{etyl|de|-}} {{m|de|Hund}}, {{etyl|nl|-}} {{m|nl|hond}}, {{etyl|en|-}} {{m|enm|hound}}), {{etyl|ga|-}} {{m|ga|cú}}, {{etyl|cy|-}} {{m|cy|ci}}, plural {{m|cy|cŵn}}, {{etyl|sa|-}} {{m|sa|श्वन्|tr=śván}}, genitive ''śúnaḥ'', {{etyl|ae|-}} {{m|ae|sc=Avst|𐬯𐬞𐬁|tr=spā}}, genitive plural ''sunam'', {{etyl|xcl|-}} {{m|xcl|շուն}}, {{etyl|grc|-}} {{m|grc|κύων}}, {{etyl|la|-}} {{m|la|canis}} ({{etyl|it|-}} {{m|it|cane}}, {{etyl|pt|-}} {{m|pt|cão}}, {{etyl|fr|-}} {{m|fr|chien}})
